.TH DOTS-ENCRYPT 1 "2026-08-28" "dots" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dots\-encrypt \- hybrid\-encrypt a plaintext file as paired age artifacts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dots\-encrypt
.I plaintext\-file
.RI [ output.age ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dots\-encrypt
encrypts one plaintext file into
.B two
age artifacts that encrypt the same content, so that either one alone can
restore it:
.TP
.I output.age
Encrypted to the public keys listed in
.IR secrets/recipients.txt .
This is the copy used on established machines, which hold the matching age
identity at
.IR ~/.age/keys.txt .
.TP
.I output.phrase.age
Encrypted with an interactive passphrase
.RB ( age \-p ).
This is the copy used on a fresh machine that has no age identity yet, for
example a
.BR curl "| " bash
bootstrap.
.PP
The age file format itself cannot combine a recipient stanza with a passphrase
stanza in one file (no CLI can produce such a file), which is why two separate
artifacts are written instead of one.
.PP
The encryption runs in three stages, each of which guards against leaving bad
ciphertext behind:
.IP \(bu 3
Recipient encryption with
.B "age \-R"
runs first, so all passphrase prompting is confined to the final stage.
.IP \(bu 3
Passphrase encryption runs second. An exit trap removes the partial passphrase
artifact whenever the script exits during this step, and a failed passphrase
encryption (a non\-zero exit from
.BR "age \-p" )
removes the recipient (key\-only) copy as well. A signal\-driven abort, such
as Ctrl\-C
at the
.B age \-p
prompt \- is the exception: the trap still removes the passphrase copy, but the
removal of the recipient copy belongs to the failure branch, which a signal
skips, so the key\-only artifact from stage 1 does survive an interrupt. Only a
non\-zero exit takes it away.
.IP \(bu 3
Both artifacts are then verified by decrypting them on the spot and comparing
the result byte\-for\-byte against the original with
.BR diff \-q .
Any mismatch or decrypt failure deletes
.B both
artifacts and exits non\-zero, so a bad ciphertext is never committed.
.PP
If verification succeeds, the two artifacts are staged and committed to the
repository automatically (see
.B EXIT STATUS
and
.BR DIAGNOSTICS ).
.SH OPTIONS
No traditional options are accepted; both arguments are positional.
.TP
.I plaintext\-file
Path to the file to encrypt. Must exist as a regular file. The file is never
modified or deleted by this script.
.TP
.I output.age
Optional destination for the recipient\-encrypted artifact. The passphrase copy
is always written next to it, with the
.B .age
suffix replaced by
.BR .phrase.age :
for an output of
.I foo.age
the copy is
.IR foo.phrase.age .
If the argument does not end in
.BR .age ,
the suffix is appended.
.PP
If the second argument is omitted, the destination is inferred from the input
path:
.TP
.B ~/.ssh/*
.IR secrets/ssh/<relative\-path>.age
.TP
.B ~/.config/tokens/*
.IR secrets/tokens/<relative\-path>.age
.PP
If the input lives under neither directory, the script prints the prompt
.B "Could not infer target. Enter output path:"
and reads the answer from standard input; an empty answer is a fatal error.
Because the answer is read from standard input, piping input into the script
can supply it. (The prompt itself is written to standard error, and bash's
.B read
only displays it when standard input is a terminal.)
.PP
Existing ciphertext is never overwritten silently. If either target file
already exists, the script prints
.BI "Warning: <relative path> exists. Overwrite? [y/N] "
for each and aborts unless the reply is exactly
.B y
or
.BR Y .
There is no flag to bypass this prompt; it is what stands between a wrong
target and the loss of the mapping between a committed artifact and the key it
was made from.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Used to resolve the two auto\-infer source roots
.RB ( ~/.ssh ,
.BR ~/.config/tokens )
and the age identity path
.RI ( ~/.age/keys.txt ).
There are no dotfiles\-specific environment variables.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Affects the location of the two
.BR mktemp (1)
scratch files used for round\-trip verification. They are removed on exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Both artifacts were written, and the auto\-commit either succeeded or was
skipped with a warning (commit failures are not fatal). If
.I ~/.age/keys.txt
is absent on this machine, the age\-key round\-trip verification is skipped
(see
.BR FILES ),
so exit status 0 can mean only the passphrase artifact was verified.
.TP
.B 1
Any of: no arguments at all (a usage line is printed; a third or later argument
is silently ignored, not an error); input file missing or not a regular file;
.B age
not on
.BR PATH ;
.I secrets/recipients.txt
missing; no output path inferable and none entered at the prompt; decline of
an overwrite prompt; recipient encryption failure; passphrase encryption
failure or abort; either verification (decrypt or
.BR diff ) " failure."
.SH FILES
.TP
.I secrets/recipients.txt
Age public keys of every machine that may decrypt. Required to exist; without
it the script exits before doing any work, advising
.B "age\-keygen \-o ~/.age/keys.txt"
followed by adding the public key to this file.
.TP
.I ~/.age/keys.txt
Age identity used to verify the recipient artifact. If it is absent the
age\-key verification is
.B skipped
with a warning suggesting that
.BR dots\-decrypt (1)
be run on a machine that has the identity; the passphrase artifact is still
verified. The private key itself must never be committed to the repository.
.TP
.I secrets/ssh/*, secrets/tokens/*
Default output locations when the input is under
.B ~/.ssh
or
.BR ~/.config/tokens .
Target locations mirror what
.BR dots\-decrypt (1)
maps back on install.
.TP
.I <output>.age, <output>.phrase.age
The two written artifacts. The script sets
.B "umask 077"
before doing any work, so both are created with mode
.BR 600 .
That is the counterpart to the modes
.BR dots\-decrypt (1)
enforces on the plaintext it writes back (0600, into a 0700 directory) and to
the mode
.BR dots\-verify (1)
checks private keys for\(em it reports a key that is not
.BR 600 ,
but it changes no modes; the enforcement on install is
.BR dots\-decrypt (1) 's.
The 600 mode does not travel with the ciphertext: git records only the
executable bit (the artifacts are committed as 100644), so a fresh clone
checks them out as 0666 & ~umask \-\- 0644 under a normal umask \-\- on every
machine other than the one where
.B dots\-encrypt
ran. Group\- and world\-readable ciphertext in a clone is therefore possible;
what keeps the plaintext private is that only a holder of the age identity or
the passphrase can decrypt, and that
.B dots\-decrypt (1)
re\-imposes 0600 when it writes the material back to its target.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Progress and error messages go to standard output. The two interactive prompts
(the overwrite prompt and the
.B "Could not infer target"
prompt) are written to standard error by bash's
.BR read (1p),
which also only displays them when standard input is a terminal; their answers
are always read from standard input. Notable messages:
.TP
.B "Warning: <relative path> exists. Overwrite? [y/N]"
Both artifacts' paths are checked before any encryption; the second prompt
only appears if the first is accepted. Any answer other than
.B y
or
.B Y
aborts with
.BR "Aborted." " and exit status 1."
.TP
.B "Warning: No ~/.age/keys.txt on this machine \(em skipping age\-key verify."
The identity is not present here, so only the passphrase copy was round\-trip
checked.
.TP
.B "Warning: could not stage <file> \(em commit manually."
.TP
.B "Warning: files encrypted, but commit failed \(em commit manually."
The auto\-commit is deliberately non\-fatal: on a fresh machine without a
configured git identity, having already written the ciphertext must not fail
the script.
.SH EXAMPLES
Encrypt an SSH private key, letting the script infer
.IR secrets/ssh/id_ed25519.age
plus its passphrase copy:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&./scripts/encrypt.sh ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
.fi
.RE
.PP
Encrypt a token file to an explicit destination (the
.B .age
suffix is appended if missing):
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&./scripts/encrypt.sh ~/.config/tokens/github.pat secrets/tokens/github.pat.age
.fi
.RE
.PP
Via the
.BR just (1)
wrapper:
.PP
.RS
.nf
just encrypt "$HOME/.ssh/id_ed25519"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The recipe interpolates its argument inside double quotes
.RB ( ./scripts/encrypt.sh\ \[dq]{{\ FILE\ }}\[dq] ),
so
.BR just (1)
performs no tilde expansion and the argument reaches the script literally:
a leading
.B ~
is not expanded (by the recipe shell, either) and the run fails with
.BR "Error: File not found" .
Supply a path that is already expanded \(em as above, or repo\-relative \(em
rather than a bare tilde path.
.PP
Each run asks for the passphrase
.B three
times:
.B age \-p
prompts for it twice (entry, then confirmation) while encrypting the
.B .phrase.age
copy, and a third prompt is needed when that copy is decrypted again during
verification.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dots\-decrypt (1),
.BR dots\-setup (1),
.BR dots\-verify (1),
.BR age (1),
.BR age\-keygen (1)